so rule 15 (same repo different areas → parallel). And the CI check work (scanner implementation) is independent engineering. Parallel is defensible and shows understanding of rule 13.\n\nHmm, but wait — actually, hmm. One more consideration: E003's check must run on serving-stack builds. It can be developed and even merged before remediation completes (it would fail builds until remediation lands — you'd add it as non-gating first, then flip to gating). The strategy says \"a gating check fails any serving-stack build that reintroduces non-validated crypto usage\" — \"reintroduces\" implies post-remediation activation. I'll note activation timing in the epic. Parallel development, AC-gated activation. OK going with parallel.\n\n- E004 Evidence package (P0, HLR-2) — depends on E001, E002 (aggregates their deliverables: module inventory, test results). Also include build attestations via AC (from Konflux pipeline — no edge). Hmm — should E004 also depend on E003 (CI enforcement evidence)? HLR-2's package = \"module inventory, build attestations, test results\" — CI check isn't part of it. No edge to E003.\n\nWait, now reconsider edges E001→E004, E002→E004 vs rule 13 content clause. Test results are not build-pipeline outputs; they're the remediation epics' deliverables. The evidence package's deliverable (the package) is composed of them. I'll model edges. Actually hmm, hold on — let me reconsider rule 13's content clause once more: \"The same holds for any build-pipeline output another epic consumes as CONTENT — signed attestations, SBOMs, provenance, scan results\". Test results from cluster validation aren't build-pipeline outputs. Module inventory isn't either. So edges are fine for those. Build attestations ARE build-pipeline outputs → AC on E004 (\"package includes signed build attestations from the Konflux pipelines\"), no edge (there's no pipeline epic anyway). Good, consistent.\n\n- E005 Documentation (docs-authoring, P0 via exception, HLR-4) — Rule 11: block