d submitted through the security review board to the external certification lab | RHAISTRAT-9013-E004 |\n| HLR-3 | P1 | CI gating check fails any serving-stack build reintroducing non-validated crypto | RHAISTRAT-9013-E003 |\n| HLR-4 | P2 | Documentation states the FIPS support posture and cluster prerequisites | RHAISTRAT-9013-E005 |\n\nAll four HLRs are covered; none is orphaned. No epic maps to HLRs at multiple\npriority levels, so no bundling flags are needed: HLR-3 (P1) is a distinct,\nindependently valuable capability on a separate surface (build-pipeline CI\nconfiguration) from the HLR-1 image remediation, and HLR-4 (P2) is substantive\ncontent authoring, which is a separate work stream by Rule 5's work-product\nboundary — both are split, not bundled.\n\n## Health Warnings\n\n1. **Missing strategy sections (non-blocking).** The strategy has no Impacted\n   Teams table, no per-component change descriptions or owners, no Dependencies\n   table, no Given/When/Then acceptance criteria, and no Non-Functional\n   Requirements. Decomposition proceeded from the HLR table, the Affected\n   Components list, and the problem statement.\n2. **Team assignments are assumptions.** KServe epics are assigned to RHOAI\n   Serving (corroborated by the KServe architecture-context file, which lists\n   RHOAI Serving as owning team). No source names an owner for Platform Operator\n   or for documentation; E002 is assigned to RHOAI Platform Operators and E005 to\n   RHOAI Serving as best-effort defaults. Verify before scheduling.\n3. **\"Serving data plane\" scope ambiguity (scope trap).** The problem statement\n   says \"KServe components and the serving data plane link non-validated crypto\n   libraries\", but the Affected Components list contains only KServe and\n   Platform Operator. The KServe architecture-context file names Serving\n   Runtimes (vLLM, OpenVINO, Caikit) as the model-execution data plane. If those\n   runtime images also link non-validated crypto, additional remediation epics\n   are required — confirm the intended boundary with the strategy author before\n   executing.\n4. **Platform Operator has no architecture-context file.** It is treated as an\n   existing platform component (it is in the canonical RHAI component list and\n   is the component that installs the stack) rather than triggering the\n   new-component onboarding chain: this strategy hardens images of existing\n   components and describes no provisioning. Its `architecture_claims` signal is\n   scored 0 (no context file covers it). Recommend adding an architecture-context\n   file for Platform Operator.\n5. **External process gates are not modeled as work.** The accredited lab's\n   historical six-to-nine-month turnaround and the security review board's\n   cadence are externally controlled schedule constraints. They are recorded as\n   risks on the evidence-package epic, not as epics, DAG edges, or acceptance\n   criteria — the engineering-controlled completion point is a complete package\n   submitted through the review board to the lab and triaged. The certification\n   decision itself is outside the strategy's control.\n6. **E001 may run above the ~2-week epic-size guideline.** It deliberately\n   bundles the crypto-linkage audit and the remediation of all KServe images as\n   one logical change (splitting would fragment a single mechanical pass over\n   one surface and duplicate the audit). Re-split trigger: re-split E001 along\n   image or sub-system lines if the audit finds materially more affected images\n   or crypto-linkage sites than the strategy's \"several images\" estimate\n   suggests. The same trigger applies to E002.\n\n## Tiered Delivery\n\nThe external dependency here (the accredited certification lab) is a process\ngate, not an upstream code dependency, so it does not create an upstream epic or\na DAG split. Tiered value still applies:\n\n- **Tier 1 (independent of lab timing):** E001, E002 (FIPS-capable images),\n  E003 (regression protection), and E005 (documented posture) all deliver user\n  value — serving-stack images that run correctly on FIPS-enabled clusters —\n  whether or not certification has completed.\n- **Tier 2 (externally gated):** E004's submission and the eventual certification\n  outcome depend on lab and review-board scheduling that engineering cannot\n  compress. The decomposition isolates this in E004 so Tier 1 work is never\n  blocked by it.\n"}}],"model":"rits/zai-org/glm-5-3","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":43021,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-8ca1c42f513e3b4b","agent_id":"a499bb043a75f3f2e","session_id":"06dfd5f6-e298-413b-a31d-879a10490586","uuid":"ce1832b0-141e-4e33-b3cf-f5b16daf0242","timestamp":"2026-10-25T17:50:58.843Z","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Decompose RHAISTRAT-9013"}
