{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-8bd9d25c5ffabef5","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-9013\n3\tepic_count: 5\n4\tcritical_path_length: 3\n5\tdecompose_complete: true\n6\trevised: false\n7\ttriage: null\n8\ttriage_rationale: null\n9\t---\n10\t# Decomposition: RHAISTRAT-9013 — FIPS 140-3 compliance for the model serving stack\n11\t\n12\t## Epic List\n13\t\n14\t| ID | Title | Type | Team | Priority |\n15\t|-------|-------|-----|------|----------|\n16\t| RHAISTRAT-9013-E001 | Eliminate non-validated crypto in KServe serving-stack images | Implementation | RHOAI Serving | P0 |\n17\t| RHAISTRAT-9013-E002 | Eliminate non-validated crypto in Platform Operator images | Implementation | RHOAI Platform Operators | P0 |\n18\t| RHAISTRAT-9013-E003 | CI gating check for FIPS crypto regressions in serving-stack builds | Implementation | RHOAI Serving | P1 |\n19\t| RHAISTRAT-9013-E004 | FIPS 140-3 compliance evidence package and submission | Implementation | RHOAI Serving | P0 |\n20\t| RHAISTRAT-9013-E005 | Document the serving-stack FIPS support posture | Implementation (docs-authoring) | RHOAI Serving | P0 |\n21\t\n22\tPriorities: E001/E002 inherit P0 from HLR-1; E004 is P0 from HLR-2. E003 stays P1\n23\t(HLR-3): it enables only the documentation epic, and edges into a `docs-authoring`\n24\tepic do not trigger priority inheritance. E005's P0 comes from the\n25\t`docs-authoring` priority exception — the strategy frontmatter priority is\n26\tCritical, which maps to P0 — not from HLR-4's P2 label (see Health Warnings).\n27\t\n28\t## Dependency DAG\n29\t\n30\t```mermaid\n31\tgraph TD\n32\t    E001[RHAISTRAT-9013-E001 KServe image FIPS remediation] --> E004[RHAISTRAT-9013-E004 evidence package and submission]\n33\t    E002[RHAISTRAT-9013-E002 Platform Operator image FIPS remediation] --> E004\n34\t    E001 --> E005[RHAISTRAT-9013-E005 FIPS documentation]\n35\t    E002 --> E005\n36\t    E003[RHAISTRAT-9013-E003 CI FIPS regression gate] --> E005\n37\t    E004 --> E005\n38\t```\n39\t\n40\t## DAG Justification\n41\t\n42\t| Edge | Rule | Rationale |\n43\t|------|------|-----------|\n44\t| E001 → E004 | Rule 13 | The evidence package is assembled from deliverables the KServe remediation produces (per-image module inventory, FIPS-cluster test results). It cannot be complete before those artifacts exist, so the consumer is serial after the producer. |\n45\t| E002 → E004 | Rule 13 | Same: the operator-image remediation's inventory and test results are content of the evidence package. |\n46\t| E001 → E005 | Rule 11 | `docs-authoring` epic blocked by every Implementation epic; docs describe what was built. |\n47\t| E002 → E005 | Rule 11 | Same. |\n48\t| E003 → E005 | Rule 11 | Same. |\n49\t| E004 → E005 | Rule 11 | Same. Documentation of the FIPS posture also reflects the submitted evidence package. |\n50\t| E001 ∥ E002 (no edge) | Rule 14 | Different components in different repos, no shared artifacts — remediation work proceeds in parallel. |\n51\t| E003 ∥ E001/E002 (no edge) | Rule 13 | The regression check's validated-module list is a configuration reference filled in at activation time; the coupling is expressed as acceptance criteria on the check (its detection rules must cover every class of non-validated usage found by the serving-stack image audit), never as a DAG edge. |\n52\t| E004 ∥ build pipelines (no edge) | Rule 13 | Build attestations consumed as content: the evidence package carries an AC that it includes the signed attestations produced by the serving-stack build pipelines, not a dependency on any pipeline epic. |\n53\t| E003 ∥ E004 (no edge) | Rule 14 | Independent deliverables; HLR-2's package does not include CI-enforcement evidence. |\n54\t\n55\t## HLR Traceability Matrix\n56\t\n57\t| HLR | Priority | Requirement (abridged) | Epic(s) |\n58\t|-----|----------|------------------------|---------|\n59\t| HLR-1 | P0 | All serving-stack images run on FIPS-enabled clusters using validated crypto modules | RHAISTRAT-9013-E001, RHAISTRAT-9013-E002 |\n60\t| HLR-2 | P0 | Compliance evidence package assembled and submitted through the security review board to the external certification lab | RHAISTRAT-9013-E004 |\n61\t| HLR-3 | P1 | CI gating check fails any serving-stack build reintroducing non-validated crypto | RHAISTRAT-9013-E003 |\n62\t| HLR-4 | P2 | Documentation states the FIPS support posture and cluster prerequisites | RHAISTRAT-9013-E005 |\n63\t\n64\tAll four HLRs are covered; none is orphaned. No epic maps to HLRs at multiple\n65\tpriority levels, so no bundling flags are needed: HLR-3 (P1) is a distinct,\n66\tindependently valuable capability on a separate surface (build-pipeline CI\n67\tconfiguration) from the HLR-1 image remediation, and HLR-4 (P2) is substantive\n68\tcontent authoring, which is a separate work stream by Rule 5's work-product\n69\tboundary — both are split, not bundled.\n70\t\n71\t## Health Warnings\n72\t\n73\t1. **Missing strategy sections (non-blocking).** The strategy has no Impacted\n74\t   Teams table, no per-component change descriptions or owners, no Dependencies\n75\t   table, no Given/When/Then acceptance criteria, and no Non-Functional\n76\t   Requirements. Decomposition proceeded from the HLR table, the Affected\n77\t   Components list, and the problem statement.\n78\t2. **Team assignments are assumptions.** KServe epics are assigned to RHOAI\n79\t   Serving (corroborated by the KServe architecture-context file, which lists\n80\t   RHOAI Serving as owning team). No source names an owner for Platform Operator\n81\t   or for documentation; E002 is assigned to RHOAI Platform Operators and E005 to\n82\t   RHOAI Serving as best-effort defaults. Verify before scheduling.\n83\t3. **\"Serving data plane\" scope ambiguity (scope trap).** The problem statement\n84\t   says \"KServe components and the serving data plane link non-validated crypto\n85\t   libraries\", but the Affected Components list contains only KServe and\n86\t   Platform Operator. The KServe architecture-context file names Serving\n87\t   Runtimes (vLLM, OpenVINO, Caikit) as the model-execution data plane. If those\n88\t   runtime images also link non-validated crypto, additional remediation epics\n89\t   are required — confirm the intended boundary with the strategy author before\n90\t   executing.\n91\t4. **Platform Operator has no architecture-context file.** It is treated as an\n92\t   existing platform component (it is in the canonical RHAI component list and\n93\t   is the component that installs the stack) rather than triggering the\n94\t   new-component onboarding chain: this strategy hardens images of existing\n95\t   components and describes no provisioning. Its `architecture_claims` signal is\n96\t   scored 0 (no context file covers it). Recommend adding an architecture-context\n97\t   file for Platform Operator.\n98\t5. **External process gates are not modeled as work.** The accredited lab's\n99\t   historical six-to-nine-month turnaround and the security review board's\n100\t   cadence are externally controlled schedule constraints. They are recorded as\n101\t   risks on the evidence-package epic, not as epics, DAG edges, or acceptance\n102\t   criteria — the engineering-controlled completion point is a complete package\n103\t   submitted through the review board to the lab and triaged. The certification\n104\t   decision itself is outside the strategy's control.\n105\t6. **E001 may run above the ~2-week epic-size guideline.** It deliberately\n106\t   bundles the crypto-linkage audit and the remediation of all KServe images as\n107\t   one logical change (splitting would fragment a single mechanical pass over\n108\t   one surface and duplicate the audit). Re-split trigger: re-split E001 along\n109\t   image or sub-system lines if the audit finds materially more affected images\n110\t   or crypto-linkage sites than the strategy's \"several images\" estimate\n111\t   suggests. The same trigger applies to E002.\n112\t\n113\t## Tiered Delivery\n114\t\n115\tThe external dependency here (the accredited certification lab) is a process\n116\tgate, not an upstream code dependency, so it does not create an upstream epic or\n117\ta DAG split. Tiered value still applies:\n118\t\n119\t- **Tier 1 (independent of lab timing):** E001, E002 (FIPS-capable images),\n120\t  E003 (regression protection), and E005 (documented posture) all deliver user\n121\t  value — serving-stack images that run correctly on FIPS-enabled clusters —\n122\t  whether or not certification has completed.\n123\t- **Tier 2 (externally gated):** E004's submission and the eventual certification\n124\t  outcome depend on lab and review-board scheduling that engineering cannot\n125\t  compress. The decomposition isolates this in E004 so Tier 1 work is never\n126\t  blocked by it.\n127\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-874d7e082a7ef236","agent_id":"abf0dd22a7ff81c6c","session_id":"06dfd5f6-e298-413b-a31d-879a10490586","uuid":"462187e1-0c1e-4bb2-a0d2-e814f76d50fb","timestamp":"2026-10-25T17:53:13.620Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-8bd9d25c5ffabef5","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Review RHAISTRAT-9013 decomposition"}
